          "text": "---\nname: continual-arc\ndescription: Solve Continual-ARC tasks using task-id transforms, validated demonstrations, and exact single-action submissions.\n---\n\n# Continual-ARC\n\nUse for stream instances requiring `programs/<task id>.py` with `transform(grid)`.\n\n1. At instance start, inspect the exact task-id file. If present, run it on the exact test grid, check dimensions/range, and submit its serialized output before any other action. If it errors or is malformed, continue with demos. If rejected, record that hypothesis as disproven; never resubmit it unchanged. For a recurring id, search and read available earlier same-task demos/verdicts before making another guess. An unverified file or past guess is not a learned rule.\n2. After a rejection, inspect the exact submitted grid and current program, then treat the verdict as evidence against that precise output/rule. Request demos while useful budget remains; never repeat the same candidate without a changed hypothesis supported by evidence. After each demo, preserve exact input/output fixtures, update the hypothesis, and run it against every fixture, comparing full grids and dimensions. For uncertain tasks, request remaining demos before submitting; plausible visual pattern is not evidence. At the cap, label any unsupported candidate a guess, not validated learning, and keep observed facts separate from extrapolation.\n   - For repeated structures of varying dimensions, derive sampling coordinates and output size afresh from each actual grid; validate all fixtures, including changed pitches, offsets, palettes, and incomplete edge tiles.\n   - For separator/panel and sparse-marker/border tasks, compare exact coordinate mappings, separator lanes, marker rays, colors, and orientations across fixtures; do not infer fill behavior or direction from one suggestive example.\n3. Before submission, execute the transform on the exact displayed test, validate height, every row width, and cell range, then emit the complete program-produced JSON verbatim as the sole final line. Never hand-edit or retype the grid. Make exactly one action per turn: tool calls for local work do not count as puzzle actions; the final response line must contain exactly one action JSON and no commentary. Do not fabricate an action by printing it from shell or present shell output as the user-facing action.\n4. After a correct verdict, save the transform only if it reproduces every received fixture, rerun it on them, then finish. With no demos, retain only as a cached candidate supported by the accepted test, not as a demonstrated rule. If no validated transform exists or the cap ends the instance, do not replace a prior file with a placeholder or heuristic.\n\n## Apparent reset\n\nIf a task id reappears with a new test, inspect its program first. If its output is unverified and earlier same-task context is available, search/read that history—including explicit demo-cap exhaustion—before submitting. A rejected hypothesis is not a usable cache; incorporate its failure and request more demos when budget remains.\n"
